### What error do you get without the modifier?

The compiler emits "sealed, non-sealed or final modifiers expected" when a permitted subclass omits all three modifiers.

```java
package com.bank.model;

import java.math.BigDecimal;

public sealed interface Account permits SavingsAccount {
    BigDecimal balance();
}

// COMPILE ERROR: sealed, non-sealed or final modifiers expected
// class SavingsAccount implements Account {
//     public BigDecimal balance() { return new BigDecimal("500000"); }
// }

// CORRECT: add final
final class SavingsAccount implements Account {
    @Override
    public BigDecimal balance() {
        return new BigDecimal("500000");
    }
}
```

### Why does Java require this modifier?

Sealing must not leak. If a permitted subtype could be extended freely without saying so, the hierarchy would no longer be closed because unknown classes could slip in beneath it.

```java
package com.bank.model;

import java.math.BigDecimal;

// Without the modifier rule, this would leak the sealed hierarchy:
// sealed interface Account permits SavingsAccount { ... }
// class SavingsAccount implements Account { ... }  // no modifier
// class RogueSavings extends SavingsAccount { }     // anyone could sneak in!

// With the rule: SavingsAccount must declare final, sealed, or non-sealed
sealed interface Account permits SavingsAccount, LoanAccount {
    BigDecimal balance();
}

// final: no one can extend this
final class SavingsAccount implements Account {
    @Override
    public BigDecimal balance() { return new BigDecimal("500000"); }
}

// sealed: continues the restriction
sealed class LoanAccount implements Account permits HomeLoan {
    @Override
    public BigDecimal balance() { return new BigDecimal("1000000"); }
}
final class HomeLoan extends LoanAccount {}
```

### What does each modifier mean?

| Modifier | Meaning | Extension Rule |
|---|---|---|
| `final` | Branch ends here | No class may extend this |
| `sealed` | Branch continues closed | Only its own permitted subtypes |
| `non-sealed` | Branch reopens | Any class may extend freely |

```java
package com.bank.model;

import java.math.BigDecimal;

public sealed interface Account
        permits SavingsAccount, LoanAccount, PartnerProduct {
    BigDecimal balance();
}

final class SavingsAccount implements Account {
    @Override
    public BigDecimal balance() { return new BigDecimal("500000"); }
}

sealed class LoanAccount implements Account permits HomeLoan, CarLoan {
    @Override
    public BigDecimal balance() { return new BigDecimal("1000000"); }
}
final class HomeLoan extends LoanAccount {}
final class CarLoan extends LoanAccount {}

non-sealed class PartnerProduct implements Account {
    @Override
    public BigDecimal balance() { return BigDecimal.ZERO; }
}
// Anyone can extend PartnerProduct
class WalletProduct extends PartnerProduct {}
```

### How do records and enums satisfy the rule?

Records are implicitly `final`, so a record implementing a sealed interface satisfies the rule automatically. Enums are effectively final too.

```java
package com.bank.model;

import java.math.BigDecimal;

public sealed interface PaymentResult {
    // Records are implicitly final: no extra modifier needed
    record Success(BigDecimal amount) implements PaymentResult {}
    record Failure(String reason) implements PaymentResult {}
    record Pending(String expectedDate) implements PaymentResult {}
}
```

```java
package com.bank.service;

import com.bank.model.PaymentResult;
import com.bank.model.PaymentResult.*;
import java.math.BigDecimal;

public class PaymentDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        PaymentResult result = new Success(new BigDecimal("75000"));
        String msg = switch (result) {
            case Success s -> "Paid " + s.amount() + " INR";
            case Failure f -> "Failed: " + f.reason();
            case Pending p -> "Pending until " + p.expectedDate();
        };
        System.out.println(msg);
    }
}
```

```text
Paid 75000 INR
```

### What about interfaces as permitted subtypes?

A permitted subtype that is itself an interface must be `sealed` or `non-sealed`. Interfaces cannot be `final` in Java.

```java
package com.bank.model;

import java.math.BigDecimal;

public sealed interface Financial
        permits Account, Investment {
    BigDecimal value();
}

// Sub-interface: must be sealed or non-sealed (not final)
sealed interface Account extends Financial
        permits SavingsAcc {
    @Override BigDecimal value();
}
final class SavingsAcc implements Account {
    @Override public BigDecimal value() { return new BigDecimal("500000"); }
}

non-sealed interface Investment extends Financial {
    @Override BigDecimal value();
}
```

### How does this affect exhaustiveness checking?

Because every subtype declares its extensibility, the compiler can compute the complete leaf set for exhaustiveness checking. A `non-sealed` branch is the one place where the set becomes open, and the compiler requires a `default` or wildcard when switching if the open branch is reachable.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import com.bank.model.*;
import java.math.BigDecimal;

public class ExhaustiveDemo {
    public String classify(Account account) {
        return switch (account) {
            case SavingsAccount s -> "Savings";
            case LoanAccount l -> "Loan";
            case PartnerProduct p -> "Partner"; // covers all partner sub-types
        };
    }
}
```

### What are common mistakes?

Forgetting the modifier is the most frequent first-time error. The fix is to decide and declare it.

```java
// WRONG: no modifier
// class SavingsAccount implements Account { ... }
// Error: sealed, non-sealed or final modifiers expected

// FIX: decide what kind of subtype this is
final class SavingsAccount implements Account {
    @Override
    public BigDecimal balance() { return new BigDecimal("500000"); }
}
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

This requirement makes the closed-set guarantee hold transitively. The modifier forces an architectural decision at each product type about whether future variants are allowed, which is exactly the kind of deliberate design a regulated banking system benefits from. In code reviews at National Bank of India, Pankaj ensures every permitted subtype has an explicit extensibility stamp.
